# Generated from: specs/303-release-leftover-completed-execute-checkpoints-on-startup/requirements.md
Feature: Release leftover completed execute checkpoints on startup
  The execute controller previously rejected a different eligible issue list when a fully completed checkpoint remained on disk.
  Startup now releases terminal run-owned runtime through the existing guarded cleanup before applying identity rejection.

  @SCN001 @regression
  Scenario: Leftover completed checkpoint is released
    Given a fully completed terminal checkpoint for issue 290 and its run-owned artifacts remain on disk
    When the execute controller starts an eligible queue for issue 291
    Then the issue 290 checkpoint and owned artifacts are released
    And the issue 291 queue starts normally

  @SCN002 @regression
  Scenario Outline: Resumable identity mismatches stay fail-closed
    Given a <state> checkpoint for issue 290 and its supporting runtime remain on disk
    When the execute controller starts an eligible queue for issue 291
    Then it exits status 1 with stderr exactly "Run checkpoint identity mismatch"
    And the issue 290 checkpoint and supporting runtime remain unchanged
    And no issue 291 worker starts

    Examples:
      | state       |
      | in-progress |
      | blocked     |
      | failed      |

  @SCN003 @regression
  Scenario: Successful finalize cleanup remains effective
    Given the execute controller starts a queue for issue 290
    When every delivery step completes in that invocation
    Then the completed issue 290 runtime is released
    And a later eligible queue for issue 291 starts without an identity mismatch

  @SCN004 @regression
  Scenario: Failed startup cleanup does not bypass identity ownership
    Given a fully completed terminal checkpoint whose owned artifact cannot be removed
    When the execute controller starts a different eligible issue list
    Then it exits status 1 with stderr exactly "Run checkpoint identity mismatch"
    And no worker for the new list starts
